(project tuna-byte-values
 (doc "Tuna book chapter: byte values - the value store grows a raw-byte kind so text (HTML, JSON, templates, media) never travels through tree encoding. Ternary remains the only form of code. Research question: can two content kinds share one hash namespace without the substrate laws noticing?")
 (status planned)
 (convention ocaml-dune)
 (section byte-values
  (doc "Values are content-addressed primitives; the path index stays derived and never leaks into the value store. 0008 adds byte_values(hash, bytes): hash = sha256 over the exact stored bytes, lowercase hex - the same address law as tree_values, different payload kind.")
  (status planned)
  (subsection laws
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
      1. ONE NAMESPACE, TWO KINDS. tree_values.ternary and
         byte_values.bytes are both addressed by sha256 of their exact
         stored content. Readers probe both stores by hash; where the
         distinction matters (value/get, route templates) the caller
         states the expected kind. Kinds do not coalesce: content that
         is simultaneously valid ternary and wanted as bytes lives in
         both tables. No kind column on tree_paths.
      2. HASH-GATED READS, CAPABILITY-GATED WRITES. value/get needs
         only a valid bearer identity - the hash is the capability
         (unguessable, 256-bit). value/put requires the caller to hold
         at least one unrevoked grant (a write capability on the
         store); admins exempt.
      3. CAPS, NOT EXCEPTIONS. Payload cap (default 1 MiB, env
         TUNA_VALUE_MAX_BYTES) is a journaled denial answer, never a
         raised error, matching v1 prim conventions.
      4. TERNARY STAYS CODE-ONLY. No tree-language string encoding
         grows to smuggle text: a template or document is a byte value
         referenced by hash. The unary-bloat thesis is falsified the
         first time a byte form is larger than its tree-encoded twin. |))))
  (subsection surface
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
      Prims (boundary, journaled, grant-checked like tree prims):
        value/put  args = base64(bytes) -> {hash, kind bytes, len}
        value/get  args = hash[, kind]  -> {bytes base64, hash, len}
        value/len  args = hash          -> {len}   (cheap existence probe)
      JSON agent surface (Bearer):
        POST /api/value/put     {"bytes_b64": ...}   -> {hash, len}
        GET  /api/value/:hash[?kind=bytes or tree]      -> payload + headers
      Dedup: put of already-stored bytes is a no-op returning the same
      hash (INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING semantics). The 0-byte
      value is legal and addressable. |))))
  (subsection acceptance
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
      1. ROUND-TRIP. put/get round-trips byte-identical for: empty,
         1-byte, high-bit bytes, a 100 KiB HTML page, and a value whose
         bytes happen to be valid ternary (kind stays disjoint).
      2. ADDRESS LAW. For a fixed corpus, every returned hash equals an
         independent sha256 of the exact bytes (no canonicalization
         drift between the prim path and the API path).
      3. BLOAT INVERSION. The byte form of a real HTML page is strictly
         smaller than its unary/ternary tree encoding - recorded as a
         ratio in the test output. If the ratio ever inverts, the
         chapter's thesis fails and this is a finding.
      4. JOURNAL COMPLETENESS. Every value op (including cap denials
         and absent reads) journals an op row; denial rows carry
         NULL-effect per the tree_ops convention.
      5. DEDUP. Double put returns one row and one hash; storage does
         not grow. |))))))
